      "discriminative_explanation": "Observation img-k7-o1 describes folds arranged 'vertically on one side and cross over the other,' while observation img-v9-o0 describes a series of 'vertical, parallel folds' with a 'central fold.' Although the specific arrangement differs between the two observed objects (asymmetric crossing vs. central vertical), the observations consistently describe the folding mechanism as resulting from the same underlying organizational principle: material being sculpted into defined lines and valleys that interact with light to create depth. The text in img-k7-o2 confirms a uniform background across these different views, suggesting a consistent context where this specific 'folding' style (carved drapery) is applied uniformly rather than as a variable element. The claim holds because the observations depict a coherent stylistic treatment of fabric-like shapes that persists across the different parts shown, distinguishing it from random or varied carving techniques.",
      "unresolved_alternatives": "The observations do not rule out that different artists or tools were used to achieve these folds, only that the visual result is consistent enough to be described as 'the same operation' stylistically. The claim that it is exactly *one* thing in a technical sense (identical geometry everywhere) is harder to prove than a claim of stylistic uniformity."
